[Final report — What a checked CUDA lowering preserves](ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-022-D--checked-lowering-of-tix-to-cuda-on-the-gpu-machine/reference/02-report.md) · Commit `4751dda025c1`. The evaluated code is unchanged.

- **Softmax and attention:** `expf` at `src/tensor/cuda-source.ts:20` can differ from `Math.fround(Math.exp(x))`; the difference propagates through normalization and, in attention, the weighted value sum.
- **MLP:** `tanhf` at `src/tensor/cuda-source.ts:19` can change a hidden activation; the second affine layer carries that difference to the output.
- **Random programs:** `logf` at `src/tensor/cuda-source.ts:18` is a third demonstrated mismatch site. The aggregate 16/20 result does not identify which of the three intrinsics occurred in each differing program.
- **Differences from f64 despite exact f32 agreement:** arithmetic and fold steps round to f32 at `src/tensor/cuda-source.ts:11–14,108`; the f64 reference omits those rounds.

**Relation:** Tolerance is the strongest of the two proposed relations supported uniformly by the shipped backend: finite counterexamples rule out exact f32 agreement. That does not make the tolerance constants tight or universal. In one untimed GB10 diagnostic, replacing only the affected intrinsic with a double call rounded to f32 eliminated every differing word on fresh original and optimized softmax, attention, and MLP inputs. The coordinator’s exhaustive intrinsic results therefore support an exact check against the **current f32 tree**, with NaNs compared as a class, for that alternative implementation. Such a check would complement, not replace, the **original f64** budget after `split-sum`.
